      <DIV>
        <DIAGRAM>Clean renewable energy system architecture diagram showing interconnected components in geometric layout. Top tier: solar panel arrays (rectangular grid pattern) and wind turbines (vertical axis symbols) feeding into transmission lines. Middle tier: battery storage systems (rectangular blocks with charge indicators) connected to smart grid control center (central hub node). Bottom tier: bidirectional power flow arrows connecting to distribution network with demand response nodes (circular connection points) and automated switching stations (diamond-shaped control points). Clean lines, minimal styling, monochromatic with clear directional flow indicators showing energy pathways from generation through storage to consumption.</DIAGRAM>
      </DIV>
      <DIV>
        <H3>Infrastructure Scaling Requirements</H3>
        <P>Grid modernization demands $2.1T investment through 2030 to accommodate renewable penetration above 70%. Transmission capacity must triple in key corridors, while distribution systems require smart inverters and automated switching. Current infrastructure handles 23% variable generation; target scenarios require 85% capability.</P>
        <P><B>Integration Complexity</B> increases exponentially with renewable share. Real-time balancing requires millisecond response times across distributed resources. Advanced forecasting, machine learning dispatch, and synthetic inertia become critical as thermal baseload retires. Cybersecurity frameworks must evolve for millions of connected devices.</P>
      </DIV>
    </COLUMNS>
  </DIV>
</SECTION>